1
00:00:00,190 --> 00:00:02,256
Bro, just a few more tokens…

2
00:00:02,775 --> 00:00:04,935
I'm one prompt away from finishing

3
00:00:05,686 --> 00:00:08,620
I built a small open-source tool called TOMC

4
00:00:08,856 --> 00:00:09,997
It does three things

5
00:00:10,604 --> 00:00:12,518
One: smaller inputs

6
00:00:12,661 --> 00:00:13,905
It organizes your history

7
00:00:13,925 --> 00:00:15,293
around the task at hand

8
00:00:15,416 --> 00:00:15,916
states

9
00:00:16,076 --> 00:00:18,142
relations and counts become records

10
00:00:18,322 --> 00:00:19,802
the relevant source text stays

11
00:00:19,982 --> 00:00:21,991
and it all goes to the model within a budget

12
00:00:22,544 --> 00:00:24,005
In a synthetic Codex case

13
00:00:24,204 --> 00:00:29,685
input dropped from 38,786 tokens to 27,235

14
00:00:29,902 --> 00:00:31,911
That's 29.8% less

15
00:00:32,166 --> 00:00:33,090
with five out of five

16
00:00:33,110 --> 00:00:34,421
correct before and after

17
00:00:34,693 --> 00:00:36,400
In a separate Claude Code trial

18
00:00:36,542 --> 00:00:39,419
the reported session cost fell by about 38%

19
00:00:40,061 --> 00:00:42,599
Two: switch assistants and keep going

20
00:00:43,042 --> 00:00:44,882
Save a task notebook in Codex

21
00:00:45,080 --> 00:00:46,467
recall it in Claude Code

22
00:00:46,929 --> 00:00:48,599
and it recaps before it continues

23
00:00:49,189 --> 00:00:50,934
Three: move to a new computer

24
00:00:51,038 --> 00:00:52,915
Export the notebook to a file

25
00:00:53,133 --> 00:00:54,387
import it on the new machine

26
00:00:54,680 --> 00:00:55,859
and recall it to check

27
00:00:56,350 --> 00:00:58,142
We also ran long-history benchmarks

28
00:00:58,162 --> 00:01:00,190
with GPT-5.1

29
00:01:00,313 --> 00:01:04,133
Rednote preview and DeepSeek V4.1 Flash

30
00:01:04,456 --> 00:01:06,955
Across 54 long-history comparisons

31
00:01:07,267 --> 00:01:10,125
TOMC's average score was higher every time

32
00:01:10,418 --> 00:01:11,219
Versus LIGHT

33
00:01:11,305 --> 00:01:15,068
it used about 36.6% fewer input tokens

34
00:01:15,154 --> 00:01:16,917
averaged over the three models

35
00:01:17,501 --> 00:01:19,831
These numbers come from specific tests

36
00:01:20,086 --> 00:01:20,963
For your own task

37
00:01:21,181 --> 00:01:23,209
run a small side-by-side first

38
00:01:23,831 --> 00:01:26,255
The code and evaluation records are on GitHub

39
00:01:26,699 --> 00:01:27,991
It's an early research release

40
00:01:28,189 --> 00:01:29,312
and feedback is welcome

41
00:01:29,591 --> 00:01:31,845
And yes, a small personal ask

42
00:01:32,119 --> 00:01:33,864
if you've used it and found it useful

43
00:01:34,194 --> 00:01:35,505
give it a Star on GitHub
